# Fetched bars and their manifest are DERIVED DATA, rebuildable from Kite.
#
# Not committed for two reasons: it reached 1.1 MB across 134 files after
# verifying a single session, and a full replay of the 102 replayable days is
# ~4,182 symbol-days (REPLAY_DESIGN §3.2) — hundreds of megabytes of binary
# churn in a repository whose history should stay readable.
#
# The cache is safe to delete. The manifest is the coverage record for a RUN,
# and any run whose conclusions matter should have its coverage line copied into
# the findings ledger rather than left to live here.
cache/

# Frozen parameter files are the opposite case and are DELIBERATELY NOT ignored:
# REPLAY_DESIGN §8 requires frozen.json to resolve to a committed git object
# before the holdout runner will start. Ignoring params/ would silently disable
# that gate.
#
# SAYING SO WAS NOT ENOUGH, AND THIS IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE FIX — 2026-08-16.
# The paragraph above shipped as a comment and nothing else. The ROOT .gitignore
# carries a blanket `*.json` (a credentials guard, line 19), which no comment
# down here overrides, so `params/frozen.json` was unstageable and the R2
# refusal — "the parameters must resolve to a committed git object" — could
# never be satisfied by any sequence of actions. A gate that cannot PASS is the
# same defect as one that cannot fail, and this project has shipped one of each.
# The negation below is what actually un-ignores it; `git check-ignore -v` is
# what proves it, and is run in the ledger entry for this stage.
#
# The root rule stays intact everywhere else. It exists to keep service-account
# credentials out of the history, so `freeze.build()` refuses to write any key
# whose system_config row is marked `is_secret` — the narrow exemption here is
# paired with a check that the exempted file cannot carry a secret.
!params/
!params/*.json
!params/*.jsonl
